# Redirection in Linux

Redirection allows you to control where the input and output of a command go.

By default:
- Standard output (stdout) goes to the screen.
- Standard error (stderr) goes to the screen.
- Standard input (stdin) comes from the keyboard.

Redirection lets you change this behavior.

---

## Output Redirection

| Symbol | Meaning |
|--------|----------|
| `>` | Redirect output and overwrite the file |
| `>>` | Redirect output and append to the file |

### Examples

```bash
ls > output.txt
